## Why This Is Asked

Tests for client-side file uploads with offline retry focus on realism, while remaining beginner-friendly by focusing on deterministic behaviors like chunking, offline storage, and privacy checks.

## Key Concepts

- Chunking and boundary handling
- Offline storage (IndexedDB)
- Retry/backoff strategy and idempotency
- Privacy controls in payloads
- Accessibility and cross-browser basics

## Code Example

```javascript
function chunk(file, size){
  const chunks=[];
  for(let i=0;i<file.size;i+=size){
    chunks.push(file.slice(i, Math.min(i+size, file.size)));
  }
  return chunks;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test very large files near quota or timeout boundaries?
- How would you validate privacy across different browsers and network conditions?

## Why This Is Asked

Explores a practical, beginner-friendly angle on reliability for end-to-end webhook delivery, focusing on idempotency and retry semantics rather than high-scale design.

## Key Concepts

- Webhook delivery semantics and idempotency
- Retry/backoff with jitter and retry limits
- Deduplication across retries using event_id
- Contract testing for HTTP endpoints
- CI/test data sets and test doubles

## Code Example

```javascript
// Example test helper: dedupe by event_id
const delivered = new Set();
function deliver(event) {
  if (delivered.has(event.id)) return false;
  delivered.add(event.id);
  return true;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test a failing endpoint that recovers with backoff without starving others?
- How would you simulate realistic load to detect race conditions in the dispatcher?

## Why This Is Asked

This tests ability to design end-to-end, multi-tenant privacy-conscious tests for streaming data, including isolation, schema evolution, and canary risk.

## Key Concepts

- Multi-tenancy isolation
- Data privacy and retention
- Schema evolution and backward compatibility
- Streaming backpressure and performance
- Canary rollout and rollback criteria

## Code Example

```javascript
// Example: simple data leakage check
function checkIsolation(records) {
  const seenTenants = new Set();
  for (const r of records) {
    if (seenTenants.has(r.tenantId)) return false;
    seenTenants.add(r.tenantId);
  }
  return true;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you generate tenant-scoped synthetic data with privacy constraints?
- What instrumentation would you add to verify no cross-tenant leakage under burst load?
